\subsection{Structured componentwise and direct-decrease refinement}
\label{sec:stability-widening-structured}

The two-block cylinder of Theorem~\ref{thm:widen-cylinder-iss} is retained as a
closed-form conservative certificate.  It is not necessary, however, to charge
all translational coordinates or all geometry-sensitive coordinates against the
same radius.  The exact remainder in Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-nonlinear-load} contains
no quadratic self-term in velocity, position, integral displacement, latent
acceleration, or accelerometer bias.  Moreover, the integral pseudo-measurement
residual is exactly $-\delta\vct S$: velocity and position can influence a later
$S$ residual through the homogeneous $v\rightarrow p\rightarrow S$ dynamics,
but they do not enter the instantaneous $S\rightarrow\theta$ correction
nonlinearity.  This subsection preserves those two facts and then replaces the
single adversarial Lipschitz reduction by an optional direct Lyapunov-decrease
certificate.

\subsubsection{Separating the integral state from velocity and position}

Use the fixed dimensionless coordinates of
Sec.~\ref{sec:iss-block-basin} and write
\begin{equation}
 \vct z=(\vct z_\theta,\vct z_{bg},\vct z_{aw},\vct z_{ba},
         \vct z_S,\vct z_{vp}),
 \qquad
 \vct z_{vp}:=(\vct z_v,\vct z_p).
 \label{eq:widen-component-split}
\end{equation}
The held-bias mode omits $\vct z_{ba}$ from the active state and treats the
frozen physical bias error as an input exactly as before.  This is only a proof
partition: all first-order cross-covariances, Kalman-gain terms, and the complete
$v\rightarrow p\rightarrow S$ chain remain in
$\widetilde\mat A_k^{\rm cl}$.

For nonnegative radii define
\begin{align}
 \mathcal C_\star(\vct r,r_{vp})
 :=\{\vct z:\;&\|\vct z_\theta\|\le r_\theta,
 \ \|\vct z_{bg}\|\le r_{bg},
 \ \|\vct z_{aw}\|\le r_{aw},\nonumber\\
 &\|\vct z_{ba}\|\le r_{ba},
 \ \|\vct z_S\|\le r_S,
 \ \|\vct z_{vp}\|\le r_{vp}\},
 \label{eq:widen-component-tube}\\
 \vct r&:=(r_\theta,r_{bg},r_{aw},r_{ba},r_S)^\top.
 \nonumber
\end{align}
The $r_{ba}$ constraint is omitted in held-bias active coordinates.  The radius
$r_{vp}$ is retained for chart, physical, and finite-capture safety, but it will
not appear in the instantaneous nonlinear-load inequality below.

For each admitted sample let nonnegative incremental gains
$k_{\theta,k}$, $k_{bg,k}$, $k_{aw,k}$, and $k_{ba,k}$ satisfy the compact-domain
mean-value bound
\begin{equation}
 \|\Delta\vct\theta_{g,k}\|
 \le k_{\theta,k}\|\vct z_\theta\|
    +k_{bg,k}\|\vct z_{bg}\|
    +k_{aw,k}\|\vct z_{aw}\|
    +k_{ba,k}\|\vct z_{ba}\|,
 \label{eq:widen-component-gain}
\end{equation}
where $\Delta\vct\theta_{g,k}$ contains the accepted accelerometer and
magnetometer corrections but not the $S$ residual.  Smoothness on the declared
compact accepted-measurement/geodesic family makes finite such gains available.
For the integral update define the samplewise exact linear cross-gain
\begin{equation}
 k_{S,k}:=\|\mat E_\theta\mat K_{S,k}\mat D_S^{-1}\|.
 \label{eq:widen-component-S-gain}
\end{equation}
With
\begin{equation}
 \vct u(\vct z):=
 \bigl(\|\vct z_\theta\|,\|\vct z_{bg}\|,
       \|\vct z_{aw}\|,\|\vct z_{ba}\|,\|\vct z_S\|\bigr)^\top,
 \quad
 \vct k_k:=(k_{\theta,k},k_{bg,k},k_{aw,k},k_{ba,k},k_{S,k})^\top,
 \label{eq:widen-component-vectors}
\end{equation}
we therefore have the source-faithful bound
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{\|\Delta\vct\theta_k\|\le\vct k_k^\top\vct u(\vct z).}
 \label{eq:widen-component-correction}
\end{equation}
In particular, neither $\delta\vct v$ nor $\delta\vct p$ appears in
Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-component-correction}.

\subsubsection{Componentwise nonlinear budget}

Absorb the fixed scaling and the factor $c_N$ of
Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-block-remainder} into nonnegative constants
$\bar c_i$, $i=1,\ldots,5$, corresponding to the five terms in
Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-nonlinear-load}.  On
$\mathcal C_\star(\vct r,r_{vp})$, use
$xy\le(r_yx+r_xy)/2$ for $0\le x\le r_x$ and
$0\le y\le r_y$ to define
\begin{equation}
 \vct h(\vct r):=
 \begin{bmatrix}
  \bar c_1r_\theta+\tfrac12\bar c_2r_{bg}
                  +\tfrac12\bar c_3r_{aw}\\
  \tfrac12\bar c_2r_\theta+\bar c_4r_{bg}\\
  \tfrac12\bar c_3r_\theta\\
  0\\
  0
 \end{bmatrix}.
 \label{eq:widen-component-h}
\end{equation}
Then all geometry-only terms in the scaled remainder satisfy
\begin{equation}
 \bar c_1\|z_\theta\|^2
 +\bar c_2\|z_\theta\|\|z_{bg}\|
 +\bar c_3\|z_\theta\|\|z_{aw}\|
 +\bar c_4\|z_{bg}\|^2
 \le \|\vct h(\vct r)\|\,\|\vct z\|.
 \label{eq:widen-component-geometry-load}
\end{equation}
Furthermore, because
$\vct u\preceq\vct r$ and
$\vct k_k^\top\vct u\le\|\vct k_k\|\,\|\vct z\|$,
\begin{equation}
 \frac{(\vct k_k^\top\vct u)^2}{\|\vct z\|}
 \le \|\vct k_k\|\,\vct k_k^\top\vct r,
 \qquad \vct z\ne0.
 \label{eq:widen-component-correction-load}
\end{equation}
Thus the samplewise componentwise load
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 L_{\star,k}(\vct r)
 :=\|\vct h(\vct r)\|
   +\bar c_5\|\vct k_k\|\,\vct k_k^\top\vct r}
 \label{eq:widen-component-load}
\end{equation}
contains $r_\theta,r_{bg},r_{aw},r_{ba},r_S$ but no $r_{vp}$.
If the lever-arm path is disabled, $\bar c_4=0$ as in the base theorem.  A large
latent-acceleration radius is therefore charged only through the
attitude--acceleration product and its actual correction gain; a large residual
accelerometer-bias radius is charged only through its correction path; and
velocity and position incur no instantaneous nonlinear penalty at all.

\begin{theorem}[Componentwise regional practical ISS]
\label{thm:widen-component-iss}
Assume Phases A--C and choose a compact tube
$\mathcal C_\star(\vct r,r_{vp})$ whose complete one-step prefixes remain in the
accepted measurement, covariance, and geodesic domain.  If
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \sup_k L_{\star,k}(\vct r)<\ell_* ,}
 \label{eq:widen-component-condition}
\end{equation}
then every sufficiently small Lyapunov sublevel contained in the tube is
robustly invariant and satisfies Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-block-local-bound}.  The
radius $r_{vp}$ affects only proof-domain and finite-capture safety; it does not
enter the nonlinear smallness condition.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Equations~\eqref{eq:widen-component-geometry-load} and
\eqref{eq:widen-component-correction-load}, together with
Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-component-correction}, give
$\|\widetilde\varphi_k(z)\|\le
L_{\star,k}(\vct r)\|z\|$ throughout the tube.  Condition
\eqref{eq:widen-component-condition} therefore implies the Phase-B smallness
condition, and Theorem~\ref{thm:iss-block-local} applies.  No step replaces
$r_{vp}$ by any geometry radius because neither $v$ nor $p$ occurs in the exact
nonlinear load.
\end{proof}

The old two-block cylinder remains a valid fallback.  Setting common radii and
replacing the component gains by their independent worst-case suprema recovers a
coarser sufficient bound of the previous form.  Consequently this refinement
does not revoke any trajectory already certified by the existing theorem; it
adds a less collapsed certificate route.

\subsubsection{Joint mode-consistent gain families}

Independent scalar suprema can combine gain extrema that are not simultaneously
realizable.  To avoid that artifact, let $\mu$ denote a source proof mode
(held/active accelerometer bias, accepted-update pattern, pseudo-update phase,
and magnetic-gauge state), and define the compact joint family
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal G_\mu:=\left\{
 (\widetilde A_k^{\rm cl},\mat P_k,\mat P_{k+1},
  \mat K_{a,k},\mat K_{m,k},\mat K_{S,k},
  \vct k_k):
 \begin{array}{l}
 \text{all entries are generated by the same admitted}\\[-1mm]
 \text{covariance, geometry, parameters, and source mode }\mu
 \end{array}
 \right\}.
 \label{eq:widen-joint-family}
\end{equation}
A verifier may retain
\begin{equation}
 L_{\star,\mu}(\vct r)
 :=\sup_{g\in\mathcal G_\mu}L_\star(g;\vct r),
 \qquad
 L_\star^{\rm joint}(\vct r)
 :=\max_{\mu}L_{\star,\mu}(\vct r),
 \label{eq:widen-joint-load}
\end{equation}
rather than taking a separate supremum of each component of $\vct k$ and then
placing all component maxima in one fictitious tuple.  Because every
$g\in\mathcal G_\mu$ is also covered by the corresponding independent component
bounds, the joint supremum is never larger than that independent-supremum
relaxation.  This is a certificate refinement only; it does not yet restrict the
proof to the narrower source-reachable switching family considered in the next
stage of the widening program.

\subsubsection{Direct structured Lyapunov decrease}

The scalar test above remains conservative because it discards the direction of
the nonlinear remainder.  For zero deterministic input define the exact
one-step decrement associated with the same Lyapunov sequence as
\begin{align}
 \mathscr D_k(\vct z)
 &:=(\widetilde A_k^{\rm cl}\vct z+
       \widetilde\vct\varphi_k(\vct z))^\top
       \mat P_{k+1}
      (\widetilde A_k^{\rm cl}\vct z+
       \widetilde\vct\varphi_k(\vct z))
      -\vct z^\top\mat P_k\vct z
 \label{eq:widen-direct-decrement}\\
 &=\vct z^\top
   (\widetilde A_k^{{\rm cl}\top}\mat P_{k+1}
    \widetilde A_k^{\rm cl}-\mat P_k)\vct z
   +2\vct z^\top\widetilde A_k^{{\rm cl}\top}
      \mat P_{k+1}\widetilde\vct\varphi_k(\vct z)
   +\widetilde\vct\varphi_k(\vct z)^\top
      \mat P_{k+1}\widetilde\vct\varphi_k(\vct z).
 \nonumber
\end{align}
Unlike Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-block-basin}, this expression retains the signs and
directions of the actual gain-weighted correction, skew terms, and bilinear
rotation products.

For a compact structured tube $\mathcal T_k$ define
\begin{equation}
 \mu_{\Delta V}:=
 \inf_k\inf_{\substack{\vct z\in\mathcal T_k\\\vct z\ne0}}
 \frac{-\mathscr D_k(\vct z)}{\|\vct z\|^2}.
 \label{eq:widen-direct-margin}
\end{equation}
When mode information is retained, the same quantity may equivalently be
bounded over the joint families $\mathcal G_\mu$ of
Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-joint-family}, so that all matrices and gains inside one
evaluation come from the same admissible tuple.

\begin{theorem}[Direct-decrease regional practical ISS]
\label{thm:widen-direct-iss}
Assume the applicable Live UES theorem and let $\mathcal T_k$ be a compact
prefix-safe tube inside the accepted source geometry.  If
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{\mu_{\Delta V}>0,}
 \label{eq:widen-direct-condition}
\end{equation}
then sufficiently small Lyapunov sublevels contained in $\mathcal T_k$ are
robustly invariant for sufficiently small bounded deterministic input and obey a
practical-ISS estimate of the form
Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-block-local-bound}.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Condition~\eqref{eq:widen-direct-condition} gives the exact zero-input decrease
$V_{k+1}-V_k\le-\mu_{\Delta V}\|z_k\|^2$.  On the compact tube the input matrix
and the complete nonlinear map are bounded.  Expanding the additional terms
from $\widetilde G_kd_k$ and applying Young inequalities leaves a strict inward
margin for a sufficiently small input budget.  Norm equivalence of $V_k$ and
$\|z_k\|^2$, followed by iteration, gives practical ISS.
\end{proof}

The direct condition is logically no more restrictive than the scalar
Lipschitz route when both are evaluated on the same tube with the same
$\mat P_k$.  Indeed, either Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-block-basin} or the stronger
componentwise sufficient condition
Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-component-condition} bounds the last two terms of
Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-direct-decrement} and leaves a strict negative margin;
hence either scalar certificate implies
Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-direct-condition}.  The converse need not hold because the
actual cross term in Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-direct-decrement} may be substantially
smaller than its norm bound or may be dissipative.  The exact direct test can
therefore certify states rejected solely by the adversarial scalar reduction.

For computer-assisted certification, define for each mode a rigorous enclosure
\begin{equation}
 \overline d_\mu(\vct r)
 \ge
 \sup_{g\in\mathcal G_\mu}
 \sup_{\substack{z\in\mathcal C_\star(\vct r,r_{vp})\\z\ne0}}
 \frac{\mathscr D(z;g)}{\|z\|^2}.
 \label{eq:widen-direct-enclosure}
\end{equation}
Then $\max_\mu\overline d_\mu(\vct r)<0$ is a finite-dimensional sufficient
certificate.  The enclosure may use interval arithmetic, Taylor models,
branch-and-bound, or analytic quadratic constraints, but it must preserve the
joint tuple of Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-joint-family}.  Sampling alone is not a proof
of the supremum.  Numerical instantiation of these constants is deliberately a
separate certificate task; the present result supplies the sharper analytical
contract without claiming an unverified deployed radius.

\begin{remark}[Meaning of the combined refinement]
\label{rem:widen-structured-meaning}
The hierarchy is intentional.  The original two-block cylinder is the simplest
closed-form fallback; the componentwise condition removes artificial penalties
on $v$ and $p$ and separates the remaining nonlinear budgets; joint mode
families remove impossible combinations of independently maximized gains; and
the direct-decrease test avoids treating every nonlinear term as an adversarial
vector.  None of these changes alters the estimator, covariance recursion,
adaptation law, pseudo-measurement, or source modes.  Quantitative widening of
the deployed certificate must still be established by rigorous instantiation,
but the exact direct condition cannot reject a state merely because a looser
norm inequality did.
\end{remark}
